== 6. Repentance Made Easy

The sixth device that Satan has to draw the soul to sin is persuading the soul that the work of repentance is an easy work, and that therefore the soul need not make such a matter of sin. “Why, suppose you do sin,” says Satan; “it is no such difficult thing to return, and confess, and be sorrowful, and beg pardon, and cry, ‘Lord, have mercy upon me.’ And if you do but this, God will cancel the debt, and pardon your sins, and save your souls,” etc. By this device Satan draws many a soul to sin, and makes many millions of souls servants, or rather slaves, to sin, etc.

#strong[Now the remedies against this device of Satan are as follows.]

The first remedy is seriously to consider#footnote[Fallen man has lost (#emph[imperium suum,] and #emph[imperium sui]) the command of himself and the command of created things; and certainly he who cannot command himself cannot repent by himself. #emph[Da poenitentiam & postea indulgentiam] \[Give repentance, and afterward pardon\], said the dying Fulgentius.] that repentance is a mighty work, a difficult work, a work that is above our power. There is no power below the power that raised Christ from the dead and made the world that can break the heart of a sinner or turn the heart of a sinner. You are as able to melt adamant as to melt your own heart, to turn a flint into flesh as to turn your own heart to the Lord, to raise the dead and to make a world as to repent. Repentance is a flower that does not grow in nature’s garden. “Can the Ethiopian change his skin, or the leopard his spots? Neither are you#super[+] able to do good—you#super[+] who are accustomed to doing evil” (Jer 13:23). Repentance is a gift that comes down from above; men are not born with repentance in their hearts as they are born with tongues in their mouths.#footnote[It was a vain boast of King Cyrus that caused it to be written upon his tombstone (πάντα ποιεῖν ἐδυνάμην), I could do all things. So could Paul too, but it was through Christ who strengthened him.] Acts 5:31: “God exalted Him to His right hand as Prince and Savior, in order to grant repentance and forgiveness of sins to Israel.” So in 2 Tim 2:25: “gently reproving those who oppose him, in the hope that God may grant them repentance leading to a knowledge of the truth.” It is not in the power of any mortal to repent at pleasure. Some ignorant, deluded souls vainly imagine that these five words, “Lord, have mercy upon me,” are efficacious to send them to heaven. But as many are ruined by buying a counterfeit jewel, so many are in hell by mistaking their repentance. Many rest in their repentance, though it is but the shadow of repentance, which caused one to say, “Repentance damns more than sin.”

The second remedy against this device of Satan is solemnly to consider the nature of true repentance. Repentance is something other than what worldly men imagine. Repentance is sometimes taken in a more strict and narrow#footnote[The Hebrew word for repentance is תשובה, from שוב, which signifies to return. It implies a going back from what a man had done; it denotes a returning or converting from one thing to another, from sin to God. The Greeks have two words by which they express the nature of repentance. One is μεταμελεῖν, which signifies to be full of care, anxious, troubled after a thing is done. The other word, μετάνοια, is #emph[resipiscentia,] after-wit or after-wisdom, the mind’s recovering of wisdom, or growing wiser after our folly; #emph[Ab ἄνοια dementia & μετά post] \[from ἄνοια, madness, and μετά, after\], it being the correction of men’s folly, and a returning #emph[ad sanam mentem] \[to a sound mind\]. True repentance is a thorough change both of the mind and of the conduct. #emph[Optima & aptissima poenitentia est nova vita] \[The best and fittest repentance is a new life\], says Luther, and the saying is an excellent saying. Repentance for sin is worth nothing without repentance from sin. “If you repent with a contradiction,” says Tertullian, “God will pardon you with a contradiction. You repent, and yet continue in your sin; God will pardon you, and yet send you to hell. There is a pardon with a contradiction.” Negative goodness serves no man to save him from the axe. It is said of Ithacus that the hatred of the Priscillian heresy was all the virtue he had. The evil servant did not waste his talent in riotous living. Those reprobates in Matt 25 did not rob the saints, but they did not relieve them; for this they must eternally perish.] sense, for godly sorrow; sometimes repentance is taken in a broad sense, for amendment of life. Repentance has three things in it, namely, the #emph[act,] the #emph[subject,] and the #emph[terms.]

1\. The formal #emph[act] of repentance is a changing and converting. It is often set forth in Scripture by turning: “Turn me, and I will be turned,” says Ephraim; “after I was turned, I repented,” he says. It is a turning from darkness to light.

2\. The #emph[subject] changed and converted is the whole man: both the sinner’s heart and his life; first his heart, then his life; first his person, then his practice and conduct. “Wash and cleanse yourselves#super[+]”: there is the change of their persons. “Remove your#super[+] evil deeds from My sight. Stop doing evil! Learn to do right”: there is the change of their practices. So “Cast away from yourselves#super[+]” (says Ezekiel) “all the transgressions you#super[+] have committed”: there is the change of the life; “and fashion for yourselves#super[+] a new heart and a new spirit”: there is the change of the heart.

3\. The #emph[terms] of this change and conversion, from which and to which both heart and life must be changed, are these: from all sin, to God. The heart must be changed from the state and power of sin, the life from the acts of sin, but both to God: the heart to be under his power in a state of grace, the life to be under his rule in all new obedience. As the apostle speaks: “to open their eyes, so that they may turn from darkness to light and from the power of Satan to God.” So the prophet Isaiah says, “Let the wicked man forsake his own way and the unrighteous man his own thoughts; let him return to the #smallcaps[Lord].” Thus much for the nature of evangelical repentance. Now, souls, tell me whether it is such an easy thing to repent as Satan suggests. Besides what has been said, I desire that you#super[+] take notice that repentance includes a turning from the most darling sin: Ephraim shall say, “What have I to do anymore with idols?” Indeed, it is a turning from all sin to God. Ezek 18:30: “Therefore, O house of Israel, I will judge you#super[+], each according to his ways, declares the Lord #smallcaps[God]. Repent and turn from all your#super[+] transgressions, so that iniquity will not be your#super[+] ruin.” Herod turned from many sins, but did not turn from his Herodias, which was his ruin. Judas turned from all visible wickedness, yet he would not cast out that golden devil, covetousness, and therefore was cast into the hottest place in hell. He who does not turn from every sin does not turn aright from any one sin. Every sin strikes at the honor of God, the being of God, the glory of God, the heart of Christ, the joy of the Spirit, and the peace of a man’s conscience. And therefore a soul truly penitent strikes at all, hates all, fights against all, and will labor to draw strength from a crucified Christ to crucify all. A true penitent knows neither father nor mother, neither right eye nor right hand, but will pluck out the one and cut off the other. Saul spared but one Agag, and that cost him his soul and his kingdom. Besides, repentance is not only a turning from all sin, but also a turning to all good: to a love of all good, to a prizing of all good, and to a following after all good. Ezek 18:21: “But if the wicked man turns from all the sins he has committed, keeps all My statutes, and does what is just and right, he will surely live; he will not die.” That is, a righteousness and holiness that is only negative is no righteousness or holiness. David fulfilled all the will of God and had regard for all his commandments, and so had Zechariah and Elizabeth. It is not enough that the tree does not bear bad fruit; it must bear good fruit, or else it must be cut down and thrown into the fire. So it is not enough that you#super[+] are not wicked in this way and that; you#super[+] must be gracious and good in this way and that, or else divine justice will put the axe of divine vengeance to the root of your#super[+] souls and cut you#super[+] off forever. “Every tree that does not bear good fruit is cut down and thrown into the fire.” Besides, repentance includes a sense of sin’s sinfulness, how opposite and contrary it is to the blessed God. God is light, sin is darkness; God is life, sin is death; God is heaven, sin is hell; God is beauty, sin is deformity. True repentance also includes a sense of sin’s harmfulness: how it cast angels out of heaven and Adam out of paradise; how it laid the first cornerstone in hell, and brought in all the curses, crosses, and miseries that are in the world; how it makes men liable to all temporal, spiritual, and eternal wrath; how it has made men godless, Christless, hopeless, and heavenless in this world. Further, true repentance includes sorrow for sin, contrition of heart. It breaks the heart with sighs and sobs and groans, because a loving God and Father is offended by sin, a blessed Savior crucified afresh, and the sweet Comforter, the Spirit,#footnote[True repentance is a sorrowing for sin as it is #emph[offensivum Dei, aversivum à Deo] \[offensive to God, turning away from God\]. This both comes from God and drives a man to God, as it did the church in the Song of Songs, and the prodigal. Ezek 20:22, 23.] grieved and vexed. Again, repentance includes not only a loathing of sin, but also a loathing of ourselves for sin. As a man not only loathes poison, but loathes the very dish or vessel that has the smell of the poison, so a true penitent not only loathes his sin, but loathes himself, the vessel that still smells of sin. So Ezek 20:43: “And there you#super[+] will remember your#super[+] ways and all the deeds with which you#super[+] have defiled yourselves#super[+], and you#super[+] will loathe yourselves#super[+] for all the evils you#super[+] have done.” True repentance will bring your#super[+] hearts not only to loathe your#super[+] sins, but also to loathe yourselves#super[+]. Again, true repentance not only brings a man to loathe himself for his sins, but also makes him ashamed of his sin. “What fruit did you#super[+] reap at that time from the things of which you#super[+] are (now) ashamed?” says the apostle. So Ezekiel: “And you will be ashamed and never again open your mouth because of your disgrace, when I am pacified toward you for all you have done, declares the Lord #smallcaps[God].” When a penitent soul sees his sins pardoned, the anger of God pacified, and divine justice satisfied, then he sits down (and blushes, as the Hebrew has it) as one ashamed. Indeed, true repentance#footnote[#emph[Quantum displicet Deo immunditia peccati, in tantum placet Deo erubescentia poenitentis.] Bernard, that is: The more God has been displeased with the blackness of sin, the more he will be pleased with the blushing of the sinner. Those who do not burn now in zeal against sin must before long burn in hell for sin.] brings a man to thwart his sinful self and to walk contrary to his sinful self, to take a holy revenge upon sin, as you#super[+] may see in Paul, the jailer, Mary Magdalene, and Manasseh. This the apostle shows in 2 Cor 7:10, 11: “For godly sorrow brings repentance never to be regretted, but worldly sorrow brings death. Consider what this godly sorrow has produced in you#super[+]: what earnestness, what eagerness to clear yourselves#super[+], what indignation, what alarm, what longing, what zeal, what revenge!” Now, souls, add all these things together, and tell me whether it is such an easy thing to repent as Satan would make the soul believe; and I am confident your#super[+] hearts will answer that it is as hard a thing to repent as it is to make a world or to raise the dead. I will conclude this second remedy with a worthy saying of a precious holy man: “Repentance,” he says, “strips us stark naked of all the garments of the old Adam, and does not leave so much as the shirt behind. In this rotten building it does not leave one stone upon another.” As the flood drowned Noah’s own friends and servants, so the flood of repenting tears must drown our sweetest and most profitable sins.

The third remedy against this device of Satan is seriously to consider that repentance is a continued act; the word “repent” implies its continuing. True repentance inclines a man’s#footnote[Anselm in his Meditations confesses that all his life was either damnable for sin committed or unprofitable for good omitted, and at last concludes (#emph[Quid restat o peccator nisi ut in tota vita tua deplores totam vitam tuam]): Oh, what then remains, but in our whole life to lament the sins of our whole life!] heart to perform God’s statutes always, even to the end. A true penitent must go on from faith to faith, from strength to strength; he must never stand still nor turn back. Repentance is a grace, and must have its daily operation as well as other graces. True repentance is a continual spring, where the waters of godly sorrow are always flowing: “My sin is always before me.” A true penitent is often casting his eyes back to the days of his former folly, and this makes him drench his couch with his tears morning and evening. “Remember not the sins of my youth,” says one blessed penitent; and “I was a blasphemer, a persecutor, and a violent man,” says another penitent. Repentance is a continued act of turning, a repentance never to be regretted, a turning never to turn again to folly. A true penitent always has something within him to turn from. He can never get near enough to God, no, not so near to him as he once was; and therefore he is continually turning and turning, so that he may get nearer and nearer to him, who is his chief good and his only happiness (#emph[optimum]#footnote[It is truly said of God that he is #emph[omnia super omnia] \[all things above all things\].] #emph[maximum]), the best and the greatest. They are every day crying out, “What wretched men we are! Who will rescue us from this body of death?” They are continually aware of sin, and continually fighting against sin, and continually sorrowing for sin, and continually loathing themselves for sin. Repentance is no passing act, but a continued act of the soul. And tell me, oh tempted soul, whether it is such an easy thing as Satan would make you believe, to be every day turning more and more from sin, and turning nearer and nearer to God, your choicest blessedness. A true penitent can as easily content himself with one act of faith or one act of love as he can content himself with one act of repentance. A Jewish rabbi, pressing the practice of repentance upon his disciples, exhorted them to be sure to repent the day before they died. One of them replied that the day of any man’s death was very uncertain. “Repent every day, then,” said the rabbi, “and then you#super[+] will be sure to repent the day before you#super[+] die.” You#super[+] are wise, and know how to apply it to your#super[+] own advantage.

The fourth remedy against this device of Satan is solemnly to consider that if the work of repentance were such an easy work as Satan would#footnote[If you are reluctant in thoughts of repentance, be eager in thoughts of hell, whose flames only the streams of the penitent eye can quench. Tertullian.] make it, then surely so many would not lie roaring and crying out (of wrath and eternal ruin) under the horrors and terrors of conscience for not repenting. Indeed, no doubt so many millions would not go to hell for not repenting, if it were such an easy thing to repent. Ah, do not poor souls under horrors of conscience cry out and say, “Were all this world a lump of gold, and in our hand to dispose of, we would give it for the least dram of true repentance”? And will you say it is an easy thing to repent? When a poor sinner whose conscience is awakened judges the exchange of all the world for the least dram of repentance to be the happiest exchange that ever sinner made, tell me, oh soul, is it#footnote[Oh, how you will tear and rend yourself! How you will lament fruitless repenting! What will you say? “Woe is me, that I have not cast off the burden of sin! Woe is me, that I have not washed away my spots, but am now pierced with my iniquities! Now I have lost the surpassing joy of angels.” Basil.] good going to hell? Is it good dwelling with the devouring fire, with everlasting burnings? Is it good to be forever separated from the blessed and glorious presence of God, angels, and saints, and to be forever shut out from those good things of eternal life, which are so many that they exceed number, so great that they exceed measure, so precious that they exceed all estimation? We know it is the greatest misery that can befall the sons of men; and would they not prevent it by repentance, if it were such an easy thing to repent as Satan would make it? Well then, do not run the risk of losing God, Christ, heaven, and your soul forever by listening to this device of Satan, namely, that it is an easy thing to repent, etc. If it is so easy, why then do wicked men’s hearts rise so against those who press the doctrine of repentance in the sweetest way, and by the strongest and choicest arguments that the Scripture affords? And why do they kill two at once, the faithful laborer’s name and their own souls, by their wicked words and actions, because they are urged to repent, which Satan tells them is so easy a thing? Surely, were repentance so easy, wicked men would not be so much enraged when that doctrine is pressed upon them by evangelical considerations.

The fifth remedy against this device of Satan is seriously to consider that to repent of sin is as great a work of grace as not to sin. By our sinful falls the powers of the soul are weakened, the#footnote[Yet it is better to be kept from sin than to be cured of sin by repentance, as it is better for a man to be preserved from a disease than to be cured of the disease.] strength of grace is decayed, our evidences for heaven are blotted, fears and doubts are raised in the soul (Will God pardon this scarlet sin once more, and show mercy to this wretched soul?), corruptions in the heart gain more ground and are confirmed, and the conscience of a man after his falls is the more enraged or the more numbed. Now for a soul, notwithstanding all this, to repent of his falls shows that it is as great a work of grace to repent of sin as it is not to sin. Repentance is the vomit of the soul, and of all medicine none is so difficult and hard as to vomit. The same means that tend to preserve the soul from sin work the soul to rise by repentance when it has fallen into sin. We know that the mercy and loving kindness of God is one special means to keep the soul from sin, as David said: “For Your loving kindness is before my eyes, and I have#footnote[Ps 26:3, 4, 5.] walked in Your truth. I do not sit with deceitful men, nor keep company with hypocrites. I hate the mob of evildoers, and refuse to sit with the wicked.” So by the same means the soul is raised by repentance out of sin, as you#super[+] may see in Mary Magdalene, who loved much and#footnote[Luke 7:37, 38, 39, etc. Hos 6:1, 2.] wept much because much was forgiven her; so those in Hosea: “Come, let us return to the #smallcaps[Lord]. For He has torn us to pieces, but He will heal us; He has wounded us, but He will bind up our wounds. After two days He will revive us; on the third day He will raise us up, that we may live in His sight,” or before his face, as the Hebrew has it, that is, in his#footnote[לפניו] favor. Confidence in God’s mercy and love, that he would heal them, bind up their wounds, revive their dejected spirits, and cause them to live in his favor, was what moved their hearts to repent and return to him. I might show you#super[+] this truth further in many other particulars, but this may suffice. Only remember this in general: there is as much of the power of God, and the love of God, and faith in God, and fear of God, and care to please God, and zeal for the glory of God required to bring a man to repent#footnote[2 Cor 7:11.] of sin as there is to keep a man from sin. By this you#super[+] may easily judge that to repent of sin is as great a work as not to sin. And now tell me, oh soul, is it an easy thing not to sin? Then we know for certain that it is not an easy thing to repent of sin.

The sixth remedy against this device of Satan is seriously to consider that he who now tempts you to sin on the ground that repentance is easy will before long, to drive you to despair#footnote[Bede tells of a certain great man who was admonished in his sickness to repent. He answered that he would not repent yet, for if he should recover, his companions would laugh at him. But as he grew sicker and sicker, his friends pressed him again to repent; but then he told them it was too late (#emph[Quia jam judicatus sum & condemnatus]): “For now,” he said, “I am judged and condemned.”] and to break the neck of your soul forever, present repentance as the most difficult and hardest work in the world. And to this end he will set your sins in order before you and make them say, “We are yours, and we must follow you.” Now Satan will help to bring the soul to look up and see God angry, to look inward and see conscience accusing and condemning, and to look downward and see hell’s mouth open to receive the impenitent soul; and all this to make the work of repentance impossible to the soul. “What,” says Satan, “do you think that is easy which the whole power of grace cannot conquer while we are in this world? Is it easy,” says Satan, “to turn from some outward act of sin to which you have been addicted? Do you not remember that you have often complained against such and such particular sins, and resolved to leave them, and yet to this hour you have not, you cannot? What will it be, then, to turn from every sin? Indeed, to mortify and cut off those sins, those darling lusts, that are like joints and members, that are like right hands and right eyes? Have you not loved your sins above your Savior? Have you not preferred earth to heaven? Have you not all along neglected the means of grace, and despised the offers of grace, and vexed the Spirit of grace? There would be no end if I should set before you the infinite evils that you have committed, and the innumerable good services that you have omitted,#footnote[As one Lamachus, a commander, said to one of his soldiers who was brought before him for misbehavior and pleaded that he would do so no more (#emph[Non licet in bello bis peccare]): no man may offend twice in war. So God will not allow men to neglect the day of grace often.] and the frequent checks of your own conscience that you have scorned. And therefore you may well conclude that you can never repent, that you will never repent.” “Now,” says Satan, “only consider a little your numberless sins, and the greatness of your sins, the foulness of your sins, the heinousness of your sins, the circumstances of your sins, and you will easily see that those sins you thought to be but specks are indeed mountains. And is it not now in vain to repent of them? Surely,” says Satan, “if you should seek repentance and grace with tears, as Esau did, you will not find it. Your hourglass has run out, your sun is set, the door of mercy is shut, the golden scepter is taken in, and now you who have despised mercy will be destroyed forever by justice. For such a wretch as you are to attempt repentance is to attempt a thing impossible. It is impossible that you, who in all your life could never conquer one sin, should master such a numberless number of sins, which are so near, so dear, so necessary, and so profitable to you, which have so long shared your bed and your table, which have been your old acquaintances and companions. Have you not often purposed, promised, vowed, and resolved to enter upon the#footnote[Repentance is a work that must be done in time, or men are utterly undone forever. #emph[Aut poenitendum aut pereundum] \[Either repent or perish\].] practice of repentance, and yet to this day could never attain it? Surely it is in vain to strive against the stream where it is so impossible to overcome. You are lost, and cast forever to hell; you must go to hell; you shall.” Ah, souls! He who now tempts you#super[+] to sin by suggesting to you#super[+] the easiness of repentance will at last, to drive you#super[+] to despair, present repentance as the hardest work in all the world, a work as far above man as heaven is above hell, as light is above darkness. Oh, that you#super[+] were wise, to break off your#super[+] sins by timely repentance!
